WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.501
[MUSIC]

00:00:02.501 --> 00:00:06.710
The way memory works is
in terms of snapshots.

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:09.440
I don't remember the entire experience.

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:12.750
I remember moments in that experience.

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:14.010
And why is that so critical?

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:18.330
Because it tells you that what you
need to manage is not the film but

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:19.805
it's a specific kind of moment.

00:00:19.805 --> 00:00:21.820
What people most remember is that end.

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:28.590
Overall, people rate colonoscopies that
take longer but end less painfully

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:33.380
better than colonoscopies that
are shorter but end more painfully.

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:37.040
It doesn't matter how long something took,
it matters how it ended.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:39.718
They remember the end which we've talked
about, but they also remember the peak.

00:00:39.718 --> 00:00:41.634
It's something called The Peak and
End Rule.

00:00:41.634 --> 00:00:43.453
People take those two
pieces of information and

00:00:43.453 --> 00:00:45.099
that's how they remember an experience.

00:00:45.099 --> 00:00:49.034
You can think of you go to Disney,
you go on the ride, that's an up, and

00:00:49.034 --> 00:00:51.207
then you wait in line, that's a down.

00:00:51.207 --> 00:00:54.390
You go from sort of location to location.

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:58.530
It's not kind of the uniform experience,
it has all kinds of spikes, okay?

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:03.600
And it has kind of moments in which
experiences naturally break up.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:05.870
So if you think in terms of peak and end,

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:10.990
you want to break up an experience when
you're near the top of something, right?

00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:14.620
So then when I think back as
a customer about my entire experience,

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:18.470
what I've quoted in my head
is a series of peak moments.

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:22.450
A lot of companies forget
the importance of finishing strong.

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:25.910
Hotels, they'll do a lot for
you when you come in, but

00:01:25.910 --> 00:01:28.840
then when you check out that process
isn't really managed, right?

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:33.000
It would actually be smart and appropriate
for them to give you a departure gift.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.990
So remember any kind of customer
experience that you have,

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:41.530
go back to your desk after this webinar
and ask yourself, how do we finish?

00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:42.720
How does this thing end?

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:47.460
